  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "threads thread pool synchronization primitives races and parallelism",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Concurrency means multiple operations make progress during overlapping periods; parallelism means work runs at the same instant on multiple processors. Threads are execution resources, while tasks describe units of work and usually build on the thread pool.",
    "Shared mutable state creates race conditions when correctness depends on timing. Prefer ownership, immutability, message passing, and concurrent collections; use locks or atomic operations only to protect a clearly stated invariant. A lock protects state only when every access that participates in that invariant follows the same lock protocol.",
    "A synchronization primitive either protects a critical section or coordinates participants. Monitor and `lock` provide in-process mutual exclusion, events and semaphores coordinate signaling and capacity, and Interlocked performs a narrow set of atomic updates.",
    "Every lock design needs a consistent acquisition order, a short critical section, and exception-safe release. Deadlocks, starvation, contention, priority inversion, and false sharing are different performance or correctness problems and need different remedies.",
    "Use the Task Parallel Library for most parallel work. Dedicated `Thread` instances are for specialized long-lived or thread-affine work; direct thread-pool APIs and low-level spin primitives are specialized tools whose costs need measurement."
  ],
