  "accuracyFacts": [
    "Volatile.Read and Volatile.Write establish specified acquire/release ordering; they do not fetch directly from physical memory and do not make compound read-modify-write operations atomic.",
    "System.Threading.Lock is recognized by the C# lock statement starting with C# 13/.NET 9. Microsoft recommends it for best performance and to reduce locking mistakes; do not invent an object-header allocation explanation.",
    "SpinLock is a mutable value type: do not store it in a readonly field or copy/pass it by value when the same lock identity is required. SpinLock.Exit(bool) takes useMemoryBarrier, not an ownership-tracking flag.",
    "Parallel.ForEachAsync defaults to at most Environment.ProcessorCount concurrent operations; MaxDegreeOfParallelism = -1 also means ProcessorCount for this API. The limit is a cap, not a guarantee of active concurrency.",
    "Parallel.ForEachAsync awaits asynchronous bodies without occupying a worker for the entire asynchronous wait. Synchronous blocking inside its body occupies an executing worker and concurrency slot but does not necessarily exhaust the process thread pool.",
    "ParallelLoopState.Stop prevents not-yet-started iterations from needing to run; already-running iterations may finish. Break records a lowest index and allows lower-index iterations to complete. Stop and Break cannot be combined in one loop.",
    "WaitHandle.WaitAll blocks the caller, is unsupported on STA threads, and supports at most 64 handles on current .NET. Deadlock depends on the signaling dependencies; it is not automatic.",
    "ThreadPool UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem and UnsafeRegisterWaitForSingleObject do not flow ExecutionContext; do not describe this only as skipping a security context.",
    "A thread-pool task, callback, or I/O completion can use a pool thread, but naturally asynchronous I/O does not occupy one worker thread for its entire wait."
  ]
